Notable judgment calls:
- **Unit 1 & 7**: chose the candidate that further splits at a genuine clause boundary (comma / em-dash) rather than leaving a ~120+ char line whole, since a real boundary was available; also accepted the minimal rejoin-only candidate as a legitimate alternative since `long` is advisory.
- **Unit 3**: no candidate applies all three required sentence splits at once — every option leaves at least one fused pair merged, so I rejected all and supplied the missing 5-line repair.
- **Unit 5**: rejected every candidate that splits the short first sentence at "yet" — valid boundary word, but the clause is only ~65 chars, so splitting it serves no purpose under the rule.
- **Unit 8**: rejected candidates splitting at "so" — the left side ("Taken once, here") isn't an independent clause, so it fails the both-sides-independent test for `so`.
